@flexiarg meta/minimise-g-over-filled-meta-policies
@title Minimise G Over Filled META Policies
@sigils [💫/比]
@keywords META, outer loop, expected free energy, G, policy, comparison, preference, epistemic value, cost
@audience War Machine designers, AIF reviewers, certificate authors
@tone formal-operational
@references [meta/fill-meta-policy-slots meta/injury-routes-to-self-heal]
@status [status[ready] "evidence[PROOF-2b G certificates; E-outer-loop-improvement; operator ruling 2026-10-02]"]

! conclusion:
  Evaluate G for every admitted, fully filled META policy under one declared
  outcome vocabulary and select the minimum, retaining the decomposition and
  nearest alternative as the reason for the chosen task.

  + context: The outer cascade has produced concrete policy instances whose
    slots and source evidence are complete.

  + IF:
    The generative model predicts, per policy, closure or progress, useful
    blocker discovery, time, tokens, downstream effect, information gain,
    repetition, abstention, and unreviewable-output risk.

  + HOWEVER:
    Comparing task labels or opaque scalar priorities is not G over policies.
    Missing terms, different outcome vocabularies, or an unfilled resource or
    evidence slot make the comparison meaningless.

  + THEN:
    Compute the declared risk and ambiguity terms minus epistemic value for
    each admitted policy; keep typed absences rather than substituting zero;
    select `argmin G`; and receipt every term, preference source, chosen slots,
    and the nearest rejected policy. A singleton family records a typed bypass,
    not a fictitious comparison.

  + BECAUSE:
    The selected META task is explained by the predicted consequences of a
    concrete policy. This is the outer-loop analogue of tactical G while
    retaining a separate policy identity, model, preferences, and certificate.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Generate deterministic and stochastic witnesses over the same filled policy family.]
    next[Update the outer generative model from the enacted policy's time, tokens, closure, progress, blocker, and injury observations.]
